'''Kulmis''' ([[Burawi language|Burawi]]: "he who gathered") was the last king of the [[Geedi Period]] and the final ruler of the first incarnation of the [[Kingdom of Ufyen]]. He reigned during the period of the dynasty's collapse in the late 6th to early 5th century BCE.

== Sources ==

Kulmis is known primarily from [[Dalmar Period]]-era inscriptions at [[Badalga-Jir]], written in the [[Mesogean script|Echian script]] approximately one to two centuries after his reign. These texts name Kulmis as the king under whom the Geedi state disintegrated, and describe the loss of the highland provinces and the [[Buran River]] corridor in his final years. The Dalmar chroniclers used the account of Kulmis's failure to justify their own dynasty's rise as a necessary restoration of order.

A small number of [[Farta Geedi]] inscriptions from ''[[Kaas|kaas]]'' forts in the middle Buran valley have been tentatively attributed to Kulmis's reign on the basis of their position in the stratigraphic sequence, though the logographic script does not allow firm identification.

== Reign ==

Kulmis inherited a kingdom already under severe strain. The Ufyen empire at this point stretched from the [[Kushish]] highlands to the [[Mesogean Sea]] coast, encompassing diverse populations whose tribute obligations had generated repeated unrest. The ''kaas'' garrison network, extended to its furthest reach during the [[Dagaalka Buran]] a century earlier, required constant provisioning and repair.

Under Kulmis, the empire's periphery broke away in a series of rebellions. The [[Bariites]] in the eastern highlands resisted tribute extraction, and [[Dakari people|Dakari]] war bands from the [[Northern Basin]] raided through the mountain passes as garrisons weakened. The Dalmar inscriptions describe a progressive contraction: first the loss of the coastal tributaries, then the middle Buran valley, and finally the outlying highland provinces. By the end of Kulmis's reign (~500 BCE), the kingdom had contracted to the [[Lake Haro]] heartland.

== Aftermath ==

The fall of Kulmis ended the Geedi Dynasty. No successor from the Geedi line is attested. The Lake Haro region was governed for approximately a century by a succession of short-lived warlords, none of whom established a lasting dynasty. This interregnum ended with the rise of the [[Dalmar Period]] around 400 BCE.

== Name ==

The name ''Kulmis'' ("he who gathered") has been noted by scholars as subtly ironic for a king who presided over the dissolution of the state. Some historians have suggested that the name may be a retroactive assignment by the Dalmar chroniclers rather than the king's birth name, chosen to emphasise the contrast between gathering and losing. Others argue that the name is genuine and that its irony is coincidental.

